# CAFS Filesystem Information
**Document version:** v21
**On-disk format version:** 6
**Status:** Locked

---

## 1. Critical Design Decisions

1. LBA0–5 are dynamic pointer blocks, not the real structures themselves.
2. LBA0–3 are 2048 bytes each; LBA4–5 are 4096 bytes each.
3. Pointer block checksums are configurable at format time.  
   Default: CRC32C. Optional: XXHASH32.
4. Parity is the XOR of the full real Superblock block and full real Config Snapshot block. There is exactly one parity block — no separate "backup parity," since only the Superblock has a genuine second copy (see Decision 9 and Section 12).
5. The WAL uses fixed 48-byte entries.  
   `sequence_number` is a 6-byte unsigned integer, little-endian.
6. ANCHOR_DIFF uses a dedicated 4KB TLV block.  
   The 16-byte WAL `value` field points to that block.
7. The end-of-device anchor mirror is a **pointer-recovery mechanism**, not a second copy of the real structures it points to. Only the Superblock has a genuine, independently-written backup, cross-linked from both ends (Section 2, Section 12). This corrects v19's Section 12/17b, which described distinct backup copies for Config Snapshot, Meta/User Table, Function Table, and a "Backup Parity Block" — none of those exist; they contradicted ADR-006 and were never authoritative (ADR-006 amendment, 2026-09-06).
8. On-disk format version is bumped from 4 to 5 (v19 bumped 3 to 4). New critical-tier structures added: Identifier Table, Snapshot Table, Dedup Table (formalized — previously referenced by pointer only, never given its own layout section), and a per-block reference count replacing the Zone Table's former binary free/used bit.
9. **All multi-byte integer fields across every structure in this document are little-endian**, chosen to match every currently-targeted platform (Linux x86-64, Windows x86-64/i686), none of which is big-endian. Previously only stated for `sequence_number` (Section 9.2); stated here as the global rule it was always implicitly assumed to be.
10. Pointer block magic is the literal 8-byte ASCII string `"CAFSPTR\0"`, compared byte-for-byte — not parsed as a hexadecimal integer. `0xCAFSPTR` as written in earlier versions of this document is not valid hex (P, T, R are not hex digits); corrected throughout Section 2 and Section 3.

