## 4. Real Superblock

Size: 4KB.  
Pointed by LBA0 primary pointer.

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–3 | 4 | magic = `0x53555042` |
| 4–7 | 4 | format_version = **6** |
| 8–23 | 16 | volume_uuid |
| 24–31 | 8 | config_generation |
| 32–39 | 8 | function_table_anchor_lba = 4 |
| 40–47 | 8 | wal_sequence |
| 48–55 | 8 | last_mount_time |
| 56–59 | 4 | flags |
| 60–63 | 4 | data_checksum_algo_id |
| 64–67 | 4 | anchor_checksum_algo_id = 1 |
| 68–71 | 4 | pointer_checksum_algo_id |
| 72–79 | 8 | last_applied_wal_seq |
| 80–83 | 4 | root_hash_algo_id |
| 84–87 | 4 | namespace_structure_id (v21, ADR-034) |
| 88–4079 | 3992 | reserved, zero |
| 4080–4095 | 16 | checksum, BLAKE3-128 over bytes 0–4079 |

`root_hash_algo_id` was dropped in the v15→v17 transition when `pointer_checksum_algo_id` was added at its old offset (68–71) without a replacement slot. Restored here at 80–83, matching how its two siblings (`data_checksum_algo_id`, `anchor_checksum_algo_id`) both keep dedicated Superblock fields.

`namespace_structure_id` (v21) selects the path-resolution mechanism (Section 7b). Exactly one legal value for V1 (`1` = the hash-keyed, per-directory B-tree design below) — reserved now, with only one value defined, specifically so a future alternative can be added by extending the legal-value list rather than requiring a format-breaking change to add it later (ADR-033's future-proofing pattern, applied concretely here for the first time).

---

## 5. Real Config Snapshot

Size: 4KB.  
Pointed by LBA1.

Layout:

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0..N-1 | N | UTF-8 TOML text, canonical config.fs |
| N..4079 | — | zero padding |
| 4080–4095 | 16 | BLAKE3-128 checksum over bytes 0–4079 |

Rules:

- `[mount_hints]` is removed before snapshot generation.
- Within any section that mixes crash-critical and runtime fields (identified by inline `# CRASH-CRITICAL` / `# RUNTIME ONLY` comments, e.g. `[recovery]`, `[dedup]`), only the fields marked `# CRASH-CRITICAL` are kept; runtime fields in that section are stripped along with `[mount_hints]`.
- Comments and blank lines are stripped; only section headers and `key = value` pairs are kept.
- `config_self_checksum` is set to `""`.
- The stripped TOML text is hashed with **xxhash64**.
- The hash is placed into `config_self_checksum` inside the TOML text, hex-encoded, matching config.fs's own field format.
- The block-level BLAKE3-128 checksum is then computed over the final 4KB block.
- The stripped TOML text (`N` bytes above) MUST fit within 4080 bytes. `cafs.sync-config` rejects a config that doesn't, before it reaches mkfs or remount.

Config fields:

- `dedup.enabled = false`
- `dedup.algo` immutable, stored in this snapshot.
- `[buffer].granularity` replaces `[buffer].block_size`.
- `[journal]` section removed.
- `[cow].enabled` (v20) — whole-partition toggle, ADR-031. Replaces the previously undocumented `default_mode` integer.
- `[snapshot]` section (v20, new) — crash-critical: `enabled`, timeline limits, `space_limit_fraction`, `raw_file_policy`. ADR-030.
- `[data].compression_chunk_size` / `compression_chunk_max` / `compression_chunk_min` (v20, new fields in an existing crash-critical section). ADR-025.
- `[hash_table]` is **not** included — it's runtime-only (non-critical speed cache, ADR-023), same treatment as `[mount_hints]`.

---

## 6. Parity Block

Size: 4KB.  
Pointed by LBA2.

- There is exactly **one** parity block: XOR of the (single) Superblock and the (single) Config Snapshot.
- Computation is byte-wise over the full 4KB blocks.
- Parity block has no header or checksum.
- No "backup parity" exists (corrected in v20). A backup-parity computation would require a backup Config Snapshot, which does not exist under ADR-006 — only the Superblock has a genuine second copy. Superblock recovery therefore has two paths (Superblock Backup, or XOR-reconstruction against this Parity Block); Config Snapshot recovery has only the XOR path (Section 14, Track B).

---

## 7. Meta/User Pointer Table

Size: `block_size`, usually 4KB.  
Pointed by LBA3.

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–7 | 8 | generation |
| 8–15 | 8 | zone_table_lba |
| 16–23 | 8 | btree_root_lba |
| 24–31 | 8 | dedup_table_root_lba |
| 32–39 | 8 | relocation_log_head_lba |
| 40–47 | 8 | relocation_log_tail_lba |
| 48–55 | 8 | compaction_checkpoint_lba |
| 56–63 | 8 | fsck_scratch_lba |
| 64–71 | 8 | identifier_table_lba (v20, ADR-018) |
| 72–79 | 8 | snapshot_table_lba (v20, ADR-030) |
| 80–127 | 48 | reserved, zero |
| 128–143 | 16 | checksum, BLAKE3-128 over bytes 0–127 |

`identifier_table_lba` and `snapshot_table_lba` were added in v20 (format version 5). Both are critical-tier by the same criterion every field in this table already satisfies: losing either risks leak or corruption, not just a rebuild — the Identifier Table is load-bearing namespace/permission data (ADR-018), and the Snapshot Table is the only record of which blocks a snapshot is holding alive (ADR-030). Neither belongs under the Function Table (Section 8), which is reserved for structures whose loss costs speed or discoverability only.

There are three fields named `generation` / `config_generation` in this format: Superblock `config_generation` (Section 4), `config.fs`'s own `[identity].config_generation` (embedded verbatim in the Config Snapshot's TOML text, Section 5), and this table's `generation`. All three MUST hold the same value at all times outside of an in-flight Full Anchor Commit (Section 13.2). Section 13.2 step 7 ("verify generation matches") checks all three against each other, not just two.

---

## 7a. Identifier Table

Critical tier. Pointed by `identifier_table_lba` (Section 7). One row per identifier, direct-indexed — `row_offset = identifier_table_lba + identifier × 48`, no search structure required, since identifiers are assigned as a plain sequential counter (never derived from filename or content, ADR-013's invariant).

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–7 | 8 | filename_blob_offset |
| 8–15 | 8 | extent_list_offset |
| 16–19 | 4 | extent_count |
| 20–31 | 12 | unix_mode + uid + gid |
| 32–39 | 8 | acl_blob_offset (0 = none; MS-DTYP `SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR`, ADR-024) |
| 40–43 | 4 | identifier_refcount (ADR-029) |
| 44–47 | 4 | reserved, zero |

Row size: 48 bytes, chosen over the minimum 44 for 8-byte alignment.

`identifier_refcount` is authoritative for whether this identifier is still reachable from anything — the live B-tree (Section 7b) or any Snapshot Table (Section 7e) entry's retained root. Reaching zero deletes the row (mechanics: Section 7f).

Filenames, extent lists, and ACLs are variable-length and live in a separate blob region, referenced by the offset fields above. Extent list entries are 16 bytes each (`start_lba` + `length`); a contiguous file has `extent_count = 1` — there is no separate "contiguous" storage type (ADR-020).

**Directory rows (v21, ADR-034)**: `unix_mode`'s `S_IFDIR` bit distinguishes a directory row from a file row, no new field needed. For a directory row, `extent_list_offset` is repurposed — directories hold no file data, so this field is otherwise unused — to instead hold that directory's own B-tree subtree root LBA (Section 7b). `extent_count` is meaningless for a directory row and stays zero. This is what makes cross-directory path resolution a direct-indexed O(1) read rather than a search: reaching a subdirectory's tree means reading its own Identifier Table row, not walking anything.

## 7b. B-Tree (Namespace Resolution)

Critical tier. Resolved in v21 (ADR-034) — previously mechanism-only, byte layout fully open.

**One tree per directory**, not one global tree — each directory row's repurposed `extent_list_offset` (Section 7a) points at its own subtree root. A directory's tree only ever needs to disambiguate names *within that directory*, so keys stay a plain fixed-width hash rather than a full path or compound key.

**Hash-keyed**: each key is an 8-byte hash of a single path component (the name as it exists inside this one directory), not the variable-length name itself. Resolving `/foo/bar/readme.txt`: hash `"foo"`, walk the root directory's tree to find `foo`'s identifier; read `foo`'s Identifier Table row to get its subtree root; hash `"bar"`, walk that tree; repeat for `readme.txt`, whose match yields the target identifier. Each level's tree walk is a real search; each hop *between* levels is a direct-indexed read (Section 7a), not a search.

**Node**: 4096 bytes (= `block_size`).

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | is_leaf flag |
| 1–2 | 2 | key_count |
| 3–7 | 5 | reserved, zero |
| 8–4087 | up to 255 × 16 | slot array |
| — | — | (unused tail, zero, if key_count < 255) |

Each slot: 8-byte key hash + 8-byte value (child node LBA for an internal node; identifier for a leaf node). ~255-way branching per level (8-byte header + 255 × 16-byte slots = 4088 of 4096 bytes).

**Collision handling**: two different path components hashing identically within one directory's tree is resolved by confirming against the real name already stored in the matched identifier's Identifier Table row (`filename_blob_offset`) — a read, not a second stored copy of the name.

Precedent: this design's three defining properties (hash-keyed, per-directory, high fanout) match ext3/ext4's HTree directory index directly, confirmed against the kernel's own documentation before finalizing (ADR-034) — not an untested design.

**Open**: whether this node format requires explicit rebalancing on insert/delete, or achieves HTree's "no balancing required" property by construction, is an implementation question not resolved by this document.

## 7c. Zone Table

Critical tier. Pointed by `zone_table_lba` (Section 7). Divides the drive into large fixed-size regions ("zones" — exact size not fixed by this document, candidate range a few GiB). Per zone: a free-space summary and a health indicator, consumed by the allocator and by balance. Not a reverse LBA-to-owner index (ADR-022) — write-collision protection comes from the allocator refusing to hand out anything not marked free, never from a lookup structure.

**Health field has two independent sources (v21, ADR-035)**: SMART data (unchanged), and a scrub — piggybacked onto blocks balance/defrag already touch, checking the mandatory checksum trailer (Section 15a) that already exists on every block regardless. A checksum failure found this way feeds the same per-zone health field SMART already populates; one number, two sources, not a second parallel signal. `config.fs [scrub].full_drive_scan_preference` (default monthly) governs an additional dedicated full pass specifically to reach cold data the piggyback approach would never touch — this document defines only that the signal exists and where it lands, not the scan implementation.

Per-block state within a zone is a small **reference count** (v20; previously a binary free/used bit) — the number that gates whether the allocator may reclaim a physical block. Incremented by a live extent-list reference, reachability from a retained snapshot root, or a dedup match; decremented by ADR-029's cascade. Exact zone size, per-zone field widths, and per-block count width are not fixed by this document.

## 7d. Dedup Table

Critical tier. Pointed by `dedup_table_root_lba` (Section 7). Formalized here for the first time — previously referenced only by pointer and by prose in ADR-014, never given a layout section.

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–7 | 8 | hash |
| 8–15 | 8 | location_id (raw physical LBA — see peer-addressing note below) |
| 16–23 | 8 | offset |
| 24–27 | 4 | length |
| 28–31 | 4 | refcount |

Entry size: 32 bytes. Bucketed by hash (bucket count / overflow chain depth: `config.fs [dedup_table]`).

**Peer-addressed, not chained** (ADR-029): every entry points directly at a physical location. There is no representation of "File B's data, which lives inside File A" — two files sharing a block are independent, equal references to the same bytes. `refcount` here mirrors the block-level count (Section 7c), not an independently-tracked number — avoiding two counts for one fact that could drift. Same representation for both dedup tiers (whole-file and per-LBA matching, ADR-014) — they differ only in how the match was found, not in the resulting on-disk state.

## 7e. Snapshot Table

Critical tier. Pointed by `snapshot_table_lba` (Section 7). One row per snapshot. No file or metadata content is ever copied into this table — see Section 7g for why.

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–7 | 8 | snapshot_id |
| 8–15 | 8 | timestamp |
| 16–19 | 4 | retention_tier (hourly/daily/weekly/monthly/yearly/manual — encoding not yet fixed) |
| 20–27 | 8 | saved_btree_root_lba |
| 28–31 | 4 | reserved, zero |

Row size: 32 bytes. Array with a header (count, capacity) rather than direct-indexed like the Identifier Table — snapshot cardinality is expected to be orders of magnitude smaller than file count.

## 7f. Reference Counting Protocol

Two independent counts (ADR-029), not one:

- **Identifier-level** (Section 7a) — authoritative for reachability of an identifier.
- **Block-level** (Section 7c) — authoritative for whether the allocator may reclaim a physical block.

**Cascade on identifier deletion**: identifier refcount reaches zero → row deleted from the Identifier Table → for each of its extents, the *block's own* refcount is decremented independently (not a Dedup Table lookup or wipe) → only if a given block's count then reaches zero does it become reclaimable, and only then is its Dedup Table entry (Section 7d), if any, no longer meaningful. A block shared with another identifier is untouched by the first identifier's deletion — its count simply drops by one.

Both kinds of zero-event (freed identifier slot, freed block) are logged to the Recently-Zeroed Queue (Section 8b).

**Open**: whether a freed identifier's numeric slot is later reused for a new file, or permanently retired (requiring eventual compaction), is not decided by this document.

## 7g. Snapshot Protocol

A snapshot (Section 7e) is created by saving the current B-tree root pointer alongside an id, timestamp, and retention tier — nothing is copied. Every block reachable from that saved root has its block-level refcount (Section 7c) held above zero for as long as the row exists, by the ordinary reference-counting mechanics of Section 7f — a snapshot is simply one more reason a block's count is nonzero, not a distinct mechanism.

Reading through a snapshot uses identical traversal logic to reading the live tree (Section 7b), started from the saved root instead of the live one.

**Reversal**: the live root pointer (Section 7, `btree_root_lba`) is repointed to a snapshot's saved root. Cleanup is refcount-driven and partial, not wholesale — a block written after the snapshot that nothing else currently references becomes reclaimable; a block also held by a different, still-existing snapshot survives regardless of what the live pointer now points to.

**Requires** `[cow].enabled = true` (config.fs) — there is nothing for a snapshot to retain without copy-on-write; an in-place write destroys the only copy of whatever it overwrites.

**Open**: behavior when a snapshot's reachable set includes a `raw`-flagged file (which has no CoW and cannot be protected) — `config.fs [snapshot].raw_file_policy` exists as a provisional field (skip/warn/block) but is not locked by this document.

