## 8. Function Table

Pointed by LBA4.  
LBA5 is a byte-for-byte copy of the LBA4 pointer block.

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0–7 | 8 | wal_lba |
| 8–15 | 8 | smart_main_lba |
| 16–23 | 8 | smart_backup_lba |
| 24–31 | 8 | scratch_lba |
| 32–39 | 8 | temp_cache_lba |
| 40–47 | 8 | hash_table_lba (v20, ADR-023) |
| 48–55 | 8 | recent_zero_queue_lba (v20, ADR-029) |
| 56–63 | 8 | reserved, zero |
| 64–79 | 16 | checksum, BLAKE3-128 over bytes 0–63 |

Both new fields are non-critical by design, same as every other field in this table: `hash_table_lba` points at a rebuildable speed cache (Section 8a); `recent_zero_queue_lba` points at a bounded, informational log (Section 8b) whose loss costs nothing but the list itself — the authoritative refcounts it was built from (Section 7's Identifier Table, and the Zone Table) are unaffected either way.

### 8a. Hash Table

Non-critical speed-index cache (ADR-013, redesigned ADR-023). Existence-check only, content-hash → identifier. Bucketed (same bucket/overflow-chain pattern as the Dedup Table, Section 7d), config-selected width via `[hash_table].mode` in config.fs. Exact bucket-header/entry byte layout: **open**, not specified by this document — the config-level toggle and 256-bit width are decided; the physical table layout is not.

### 8b. Recently-Zeroed Queue

Bounded log of things whose refcount (Section 7a, Section 10a) recently reached zero. Two kinds of entry, tagged — a freed Identifier Table slot (relevant to the still-open reuse-vs-retire compaction question) and a freed physical block (relevant to the allocator's reclaim path) are different consumers' concerns, even though both originate the same way.

Provisional entry format (not yet locked):

| Offset | Size | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | tag: 0 = identifier slot, 1 = block |
| 1–8 | 8 | value (identifier number, or block LBA) |
| 9–16 | 8 | timestamp (zeroed-at) |

Bounded by a maximum entry count or maximum age, config-driven (not yet named in config.fs) — oldest entries are dropped once either bound is exceeded.

